Consider this C# code:

```csharp
static (Func<int> Read, Action Advance) CreateCounter()
{
    int count = 6;
    return (() => count, () => count += 3);
}

var counter = CreateCounter();
counter.Advance();
counter.Advance();
Console.WriteLine(counter.Read());
```

Explain why this prints `12`, even though `CreateCounter` has returned. Explain what keeps the captured state available and whether a second call to `CreateCounter` would share that state.

Consider this C# code:

```csharp
int completed = 0;
Func<int> batch = () => { completed++; return 20; };
batch += () => throw new InvalidOperationException();
batch += () => { completed += 10; return 80; };

int result = -1;
try
{
    result = batch();
}
catch (InvalidOperationException)
{
}
```

Explain why the final values are `completed == 1` and `result == -1`. Include why catching the exception around the whole call does not let the final handler run, and how failures could instead be isolated so later handlers are attempted.
